if there's one thing most Americans  agree on its that politics are  increasingly hostile a new CBS News  battleground tracker poll finds 84  percent of registered voters in  competitive districts think the tone  incivility of politics are getting worse  Republican senator Ben Sasse of Nebraska  believes it's part of a larger problem  in his new book them why we hate each  other and how to heal sass says the loss  of community in America has helped to  widen the political divide he joins us  here in studio 57 thank you good to be  here right wait do you really think we  hate each other there's a lot of it I  think there's some data that shows over  the last 20-30 years we've gone from 14  percent of the public thinking the other  party's evil to 40 percent of the public  thinking the other party's not just  wrong but evil you write healthy you  write about this unprecedented upheaval  that's happening what's causing it is it  systemic so I would say civil war was  really bad but since then this is pretty  terrible and I think it's not politics  are important but it's not like our  politics are really wrestling with  bigger questions than 15 or 20 or 25  years ago  it's that politics is filling a void of  the hollowing out of natural local  traditional tribes people usually  understood that family shared vocation  workplace neighborhood were the core  places where your identity was found you  know they look into politics you've got  some data to back this not just your  opinion mail absolutely yeah the size of  homes yeah so in the last 50 years  average size of a new home in America  has grown 350 percent and yet there's a  lot of data that shows if your house  gets farther and farther from their  neighbors you're less likely to know the  person who lives two doors from you  that's statistically correlated with  being happy a bigger square footage  house actually doesn't really make you  happier we also have less friends Ben  you say that we're living through a  crisis we're literally dying of despair  and we have less friends we're not doing  as much with each other and to each  other  loneliness loneliness you said is the  number one problem really in this guy at  the NIH you got a whole bunch of  researchers and scientists who see  loneliness as the number one public  health crisis in America we've had a  having of friendship in 27 years the  average American in 1990 had 3.2 friends  today one point eight forty percent of  people have either zero or one confidant  that hurt and a lot of them the  confidante is their phone there's no  doubt we're seeing a shift in  social norms we're also seeing a shift  in political norms as well and your  Republican colleague Senator Marco Rubio  said he doesn't think we're gonna be  able to go back to the way things were  the political norms do you agree with  that I think we're headed toward a  deeper darker tribal place for a time  the question is how we I named this book  them because we act like political  differences are sort of core anti  identities for people until you have  things that you're for first you're not  gonna be able to figure out what we're  trying to do together in politics  politics again I think politics are  important but they're not the first  thing they're certainly not the only  thing and I think right now our politics  are suffering because a lot of people  are looking to find a grand meeting  there because we're not reflecting well  on these core under minors of happiness  at home we talked about this yesterday  but to the extent that people have  filled this hole with politics they're  watching it on TV all the time there  could be many more examples of you or  your colleagues reaching out to each  other and saying I'm not going to treat  you like a person who has only the worst  motives I'm not gonna call you evil I'm  not gonna say that your perhaps  genuinely held view is all just some  scheme to keep you in office that more  examples of that while people are  watching might be good well said you  want to come and give us a owned problem  with pride and humility I think we  really do have a problem right now where  the Senate is being swallowed whole by  cable TV news there's a whole bunch of  people who instead of seeing the Senate  as a place that you go to from the  community where your identity is rooted  where your friends are where you're  raising kids next to neighbors and they  differ with you on politics but  increasingly we're segregating our  neighborhoods so you don't actually live  next to people who disagree with you  looks the big picture you say most  people aren't watching this polarizing  TV which surprised me but you say this  because you take on the media too so  when we hear anyone powerful and  response to questions about their  behavior attack the media in general as  opposed to merely pushing back we should  look critically at them and their  behavior who are you talking about  senators yes I mean pretty much  everybody in Washington but maybe the  politicians at the top in Washington are  particularly good at trying to just  demonize there's a lot wrong with the  media and again that's not beating up on  any of the four people in this room this  is about the economic incentives and the  way we consume media right now  it's not very economically profitable  for a media outlet to try to speak to  70% of America that isn't how we consume  anymore in the 1950s 70% of households  were watching I Love Lucy was an  important content yeah but it was shared  yet in the last 15 years the  most-watched programming in America is  Sunday Night Football hit a 14% share  for three weeks in 2014 so right now  there's real economic incentives to try  to just speak to the 1% of the public  that already agrees with you  are your legs listening to you to be  better do better it doesn't seem so we  don't listen all that well in Washington  I'm one of eight people out of 100 in  the Senate who's never been a politician  before so I'm new to this place  but politicians talk more than we listen  we should listen more than we talk and  when I'm home I live in Nebraska I  commute every week when I'm home I find  that the vast majority of people don't  really want politics to be the center of  meaning they wanted to get a bunch of  pragmatic stuff done we need to build a  cyber doctrine for the nuclear age we  need to think about job retraining for  an age of work disruption that's coming  in a faster and faster pace but you  don't want to find good and evil in  politics and too many people in the  cable news tribes and in political  parties think that anti tribes are  enough to sustain them and I think most  Americans differ all right you tweeted  about leaving the Republican Party we're  going to be watching thanks for having  me  why we hate each other and how to heal  it goes on sale tomorrow wherever you  like to buy your books

